<section class="hero">
  <img class="mark" src="icon.svg" alt="" width="88" height="88">
  <h1>Find what is tangled.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>thicket</strong> reads a TypeScript codebase and writes a report. The report lists
    repeated code and circular imports, with exact locations. You hand the report to a coding
    agent. thicket does not grade your code and does not edit it.
  </p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="primary" href="#install">Install and run</a>
    <a href="report-guide.html">How to read a report</a>
    <a href="example.html">See an example</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="install">Install and run</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">brew install alecf/tap/thicket

thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>
    Run it from your project root. thicket finds the tsconfig files itself, and covers every
    workspace of a monorepo. Use <code>--filter</code> to select some of them. Use
    <code>--config</code> to name the tsconfig files yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also download a tarball from
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket/releases">Releases</a>. Unpack it and put
    <code>thicket</code> on your <code>PATH</code>. A symlink works. macOS and Linux are
    prebuilt, for arm64 and x64.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      The tarball is a directory, not a single file. It holds the binary and the
      <code>tsgo/</code> compiler that thicket runs. Keep the two together.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="then-what">What to do with the report</h2>
  <p>
    Give it to an LLM. thicket finds the candidates. The agent decides which ones are worth the
    work.
  </p>

  <h3 id="hand-it-over">1. Run it yourself</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>Then ask an agent to read the file:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Read <code>thicket.md</code>. It lists duplication and dependency-cycle candidates, and it
      has not judged them. The top of the file links to a guide; fetch that first. Pick the
      findings worth acting on. Tell me what you rejected and why. Then do the top one.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Every report links to the <a href="report-guide.md">field guide</a> in raw Markdown. The
    agent fetches that one URL and learns what each field means.
  </p>

  <h3 id="agent-runs-it">2. Or let the agent run it</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Run <code>thicket &gt; thicket.md</code> and read the report. Pick the one finding you are
      most sure about, and fix it. Then re-run thicket and show me that the finding is gone.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    If you do this often, put the command in your <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or
    <code>AGENTS.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="close-the-loop">3. Check the work</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json before.json &gt; thicket.md
# ...the agent refactors...
thicket --json after.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff before.json after.json</code></pre>
  <p>
    Finding IDs come from content, not from line numbers. So <code>diff</code> reports what the
    agent resolved, not what it touched.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-reports">What it reports</h2>
</section>

<section class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Duplication</h3>
    <p>
      Clusters of repeated code, ranked by the lines an extraction would delete. Each finding
      names what varies between the copies.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Module tangles</h3>
    <p>
      Groups of modules that import each other, drawn as a chart. thicket says whether real files
      are circular, or only the directories.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Cheap fixes first</h3>
    <p>
      Some imports only pass through a file on their way from somewhere else. Repointing those is
      a find-and-replace, so thicket lists them before it suggests cutting a dependency.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Identical runs</h3>
    <p>
      The same source gives the same report, byte for byte. Diff two reports to see whether a
      refactor landed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="not-a-linter">It is not a linter</h2>
  <p>thicket does not score your codebase. Nothing in the report is a defect.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No pass or fail. It exits 0 whether it finds 3 findings or 18,000.</li>
    <li>The summary numbers track one codebase over time. They do not compare two codebases.</li>
    <li>
      Every finding is a candidate. Plenty are not worth fixing, and
      <a href="report-guide.html#is-this-duplication-worth-removing">the guide says how to tell</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The search is deterministic, and the judgement is the agent's. That order is the cheap one.
    A model cannot see duplication in files it never opened. Reading the whole repository to look
    for it costs the whole repository in tokens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-written-code">Written by agents?</h2>
  <p>
    An agent writes what it can see. It cannot see the helper in a file it did not open, so it
    writes that helper again. Each diff looks fine on its own, and the copies pile up.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the pattern thicket finds: the same code in files that never share a diff, often with
    the identifiers renamed. Run it weekly and compare against the last report.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json .thicket/this-week.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff .thicket/last-week.json .thicket/this-week.json</code></pre>
  <p>Treat it as cleanup, not as a gate.</p>
</section>
